  "live_records": {
    "q-2529": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2529",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If $\\frac{y}{3}=-7,$ then $y=$",
      "worked_solution": "In the equation, $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =-7 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & =3\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{3} & =-21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & =-21 \\\\ y & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
