    "q-75388": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75388",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If $-\\frac{c}{12}=4,$ then $c=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{c}{12} & =4 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{c}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{c}{12} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $c$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{12} & =-4 \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{c}{12} & =12\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{12c}{12} & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}c}{\\cancel{12}} & =-48 \\\\ c & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
