    "q-101709": {
      "dom_id": "step-q101709",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "If $-5t=-60$, then $t=$",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $t$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $t$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5t & =-60 \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =\\frac{-60}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}t}{\\cancel{-5}} & =12 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
